# The numbers `scaling.hero` reads: the 128-bit powers of ten it multiplies
# by, the powers of two it shifts by, and the edges of an `f64`, each derived
# by a program and never typed by hand. The program is `make_powers.py` of
# defect 2405's lane (batch 29), run with python3 on 2026-11-10; it computes
# each number from its definition with exact integers and fractions, holds it
# to an independent statement of the same fact, and writes this module whole.
#
# The powers of ten are Go's (`pow10gen.go` in `src/internal/strconv`, read
# 2026-11-10): for each `p` from `LEAST` to `MOST`, `pe` is the floor of the
# base-2 logarithm of 10 to the `p`, less 127, and `pm` is 10 to the `p`
# divided by 2 to the `pe`, rounded up, an integer of 128 bits whose top bit
# is set. It is kept as `HIGH[i] * 2^64 - LOW[i]`, `i` being `p - LEAST`, the
# form the scaling's first product can be read alone from (Russ Cox,
# "Floating-Point Printing and Parsing Can Be Simple And Fast",
# research.swtch.com/fp, read 2026-11-10, whose proof of the scaling covers
# these entries). Every one of the 651 entries was compared with Go's
# own generated table, `pow10tab.go`, and is the same pair of words.

# The fewest and the most a power of ten the scaling is asked for: below
# `LEAST` every number of at most `DIGITS` digits rounds to zero, since 10 to
# the 19, less one, times 10 to the -343 is below half the smallest `f64`;
# past `MOST` every one lies past the largest, 10 to the 309 already does.
constant LEAST: i64
    -342

# The most, `MOST`, as `LEAST` above says.
constant MOST: i64
    308

# The most decimal digits a `u64` holds whatever they are: 10 to the 19, less
# one, is below 2 to the 64, and 10 to the 20, less one, is not.
constant DIGITS: i64
    19

# The largest number of `DIGITS` digits, all nines.
constant NINES: u64
    9999999999999999999

# The bits of an `f64`'s significand, the one it does not store included.
constant SIGNIFICAND_BITS: i64
    53

# The power of two of the smallest `f64` above zero, 2 to the -1074,
# whose significand is 1: the place of the last bit every subnormal keeps.
constant SUBNORMAL_EXPONENT: i64
    -1074

# The power of two from which a significand of `SIGNIFICAND_BITS` bits, its top
# bit set, lies past the largest `f64`: 2 to the 52 times 2 to the 972 is 2 to
# the 1024.
constant PAST_EXPONENT: i64
    972

# The floor of the base-2 logarithm of 10 to the `p` is `(p * LOG_TIMES) >>
# LOG_SHIFT`, Go's `log2Pow10`: checked against the exact floor for every `p`
# from -642 to 642, the run around zero where it holds.
constant LOG_TIMES: i64
    108853

# The shift of that product, as `LOG_TIMES` above says.
constant LOG_SHIFT: i64
    15

# 2 to the 32: a 64-bit word is split at it into two halves, whose products
# each fit a `u64`.
constant HALF: u64
    4294967296

# 2 to the 64, less one: every bit of a word set.
constant ALL_ONES: u64
    18446744073709551615

# 2 to the `k`, for `k` from 0 to 63.
constant TWO_TO: [u64]
    [
        1
        2
        4
        8
        16
        32
        64
        128
        256
        512
        1024
        2048
        4096
